	"web_text": "Early examples of Chinese brisé fans made for the Western market feature designs inspired by Asian porcelain, which was highly coveted in Europe. By the early eighteenth century, as seen in this example, European fan makers were producing brisé fans with their own interpretations of Chinese and East Asian aesthetics, in a style known as chinoiserie. The style of the woman's mantua (loose gown), worn here as formal day wear, helps date the fan to circa 1725.",
	"didactic_label": "Early examples of Chinese brisé fans made for the Western market feature designs inspired by Asian porcelain, which was highly coveted in Europe. By the early eighteenth century, as seen in this example, European fan makers were producing brisé fans with their own interpretations of Chinese and East Asian aesthetics, in a style known as chinoiserie. The style of the woman's mantua (loose gown), worn here as formal day wear, helps date the fan to circa 1725.",
	"exhibition_history_text": "Fans of the Eighteenth Century, Textiles Education Gallery, de Young Museum, 3/31/18 - 11/2/18\nGallery 9A, Legion of Honor, 4/02 - 9/02",
